#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096379 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8f8937b-de15-5d4c-aa77-1d45595fc7af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "201a713a-83ca-5e31-9a4f-257795d7eb1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4690" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n$x + y + z = 3; y + z = 2; z = 2$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that $z=2$. Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(2)=2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y = 2-2 = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $z=2$ and $y=0$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(0)+(2)=3 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = 3-2 = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x = 1$, $y=0$, and $z = 2$. Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 1,0,2 \\rangle$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096380 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de05090a-2461-514a-971d-b83c7cea124e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution Given an Augmented Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "cc8ce97a-3bda-506f-b9d2-98ac7524583a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4691" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations that is represented by the augmented matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x_1$, $x_2$, and $x_3$. We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\nx_{1} + x_{2} - x_{3} = 1; x_{2} + x_{3} =-2; x_{3} = 5\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $x_3=5$. Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(5)=-2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_2=-2-5=-7\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $x_3 = 5$ and $x_2 = -7$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx_1+(-7)-(5) = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1 = 1 + 7 + 5 = 13\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x_1 = 13$, $x_2=-7$, and $x_3 = 5$. Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 13,-7,5 \\rangle$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096381 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52e51c9a-6bce-5ea6-a9fe-febb09f8754d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Given an Augmented Matrix Containing Non-Unit Leading Entries"}] :question/id #uuid "7b44089c-8ca7-57e5-b390-7900647e3884" :question/math-academy-id "e-4692" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations that is represented by the augmented matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x_1$, $x_2$, and $x_3$. We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n2x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{3} = 0; x_{2} + x_{3} =-2; 3x_{3} = 3\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n\n$$\n3x_3=3 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_3=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(1)=-2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_2=-2-1=-3\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $x_3 = 1$ and $x_2 = -3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x_1+(-3)-(1) = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2} (3 + 1) = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x_1 = 2$, $x_2=-3$, and $x_3 = 1$. Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 2,-3,1 \\rangle$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52e51c9a-6bce-5ea6-a9fe-febb09f8754d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Given an Augmented Matrix Containing Non-Unit Leading Entries"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bea848db-ef9a-541f-ae21-65420fcba9a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 75,-10,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093466} {:answer/id #uuid "eea79d80-0e7f-54ea-a83d-ebf24abae586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 85, 40,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093467} 